      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drytown%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from Dry Creek, which runs dry in summer.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drytown%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A single saloon, The Drytown Club, remains from a time when up to 26 saloons once lined the streets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drytown%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "State Route 49, built in 1920, saved Drytown from decline after a 1857 fire and gold depletion.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
